---
/*
  Fieldwork: the fourth offer. NOW A LISTED SERVICE.

  This page was deliberately hidden until 28 August 2026. The spec gated it on
  running Fieldwork for Quadem itself and winning two or three clients with it,
  on the reasoning that publishing first is selling an idea rather than a thing
  that worked. Ernest lifted that gate: Fieldwork is a service like the others
  and is sold as one.

  All four hiding mechanisms are undone. `noindex` is gone from the BaseLayout
  call, the sitemap exclusion is gone from src/pages/sitemap.xml.ts, the robots
  disallow is gone from public/robots.txt, and there is a services document so
  it appears in the services list, on the contact form and on /global.

  It keeps its bespoke route. Astro sorts static route segments ahead of dynamic
  ones, so this file serves /services/fieldwork/ and services/[slug].astro never
  does, exactly as the analysis pieces beat blog/[slug].astro. The CMS document
  exists to put it in the lists that read the collection, and its body is
  deliberately empty because this file is the page.

  Section 07, Proof, is still absent and that has not changed. It is filled in
  only once Fieldwork has actually won clients. Placeholder numbers and invented
  examples are both forbidden, and an empty proof section is better than a fake
  one. Publishing the page early makes that section more conspicuous, not less,
  so it is the next thing worth writing.

  Copy is verbatim from docs/fieldwork-page-copy.md. "Set it, do not rewrite it."

  The three prices look understated and are meant to. Plain text "from" prices,
  no ticks, no badges, no "most popular" highlight. They are starting points for
  a first client rather than a rate card, and a comparison table would make them
  read as fixed. See the note above the tiers for why they stopped being ranges.

  One call to action, repeated. Do not add a second competing button.
*/
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

import ServiceEnquiryForm from "../../components/forms/ServiceEnquiryForm.astro";
import FieldworkEvidence from "../../components/FieldworkEvidence.astro";
const CTA_LABEL = "Tell me about your last five customers";
const CTA_HREF = "/contact/?enquiry=fieldwork";

const contrasts = [
    ["Dentists in Manchester", "Dentists who just opened a second branch"],
    ["Companies with 10 to 50 staff", "Wedding venues with no video on their website"],
    ['Anyone with "Marketing Manager" in their title', "Online shops doing well but on a slow, dated platform"],
    ["Businesses in hospitality", "Gyms paying for Facebook ads with almost no Instagram following"],
    ["Firms started after 2018", "Factories whose website has not been touched in ten years"],
];

const arrives = [
    {
        name: "The list",
        body: "A spreadsheet of businesses matching your definition. Company name, contact name where it can be found, a checked email address, phone number, website and town.",
    },
    {
        name: "The reason",
        body: 'One line per business saying why it is on the list this month. "Opened a second site in June." "No mobile version of their site." That line is what gets your email answered.',
    },
    {
        name: "The clean-up",
        body: "Nobody appears twice. Anyone already your customer is removed. Anyone who said no is removed permanently. Anything that bounced last month is gone.",
    },
];

const steps = [
    {
        lead: "We work out who your customer actually is.",
        body: "A conversation, then a written definition you sign off. This is the part that takes real thought, and it is why the first month costs more than the ones after it. If the definition is vague, everything downstream is vague.",
    },
    {
        lead: "I go and find them.",
        body: "Public information, read at scale, filtered against your definition, then checked by hand before anything reaches you. The checking is not optional and it is not automated away.",
    },
    {
        lead: "It happens again next month.",
        body: "People change jobs. Businesses close. Websites get rebuilt. A list handed over six months ago is partly wrong today, which is why this is a monthly service and not a file you buy once.",
    },
];

const goodFit = [
    "Suppliers selling one specialist thing to one specialist buyer",
    "Companies selling equipment or machinery",
    "Recruiters working in one narrow field",
    "Commercial builders and trade contractors",
    "Software companies chasing a single industry",
    "Agencies who need leads of their own",
];

const notFit = [
    '"Our customer is basically anyone." There is nothing to build around',
    "Shops selling to the public rather than to businesses",
    "Anyone who wants one list and no ongoing service",
    "Anyone who intends to spam people with it",
];

/*
  Prices from docs/fieldwork-offer-spec.html section 06, in the order the copy
  gives them, with no tier highlighted.

  They are "from" prices rather than the ranges the spec wrote, changed on
  28 August 2026 on Ernest's call, and the spec docs say so too, so nobody
  restores the ranges later thinking this was a transcription slip.

  The reason: Fieldwork was the only thing on the site priced as a two-ended
  range. Everything else anchors on one number, "from $1,200", "from $1,500 a
  month", "GH₵ 1,500". A range invites the reader to anchor on its floor, so
  every real quote above that floor feels like being moved on, and a spread as
  wide as $2,500 to $5,000 reads as not having decided what the work is worth.
  That is a bad signal on the one service that asks a buyer to trust your
  judgement about their own market. The framing line above the tiers already
  says the price moves and why, so nothing honest is lost by dropping the
  ceiling.

  Two ladders, because Ernest sells this in Ghana too, decided 28 August 2026.
  The cedi ladder is not a conversion of the dollar one. Converted at the rate
  the rest of the site implies, setting it up would land near GH₵ 29,000, which
  is more than twice the most expensive thing Quadem sells locally, and would
  read as another company's pricing. It sits inside the range a Ghanaian buyer
  already sees here, GH₵ 2,500 to 11,500.

  Both render and one carries `hidden`. initDynamicPricing in
  src/scripts/main.js reveals the right one after the geo lookup, the same
  mechanism as the homepage tiers, and for the same reason: public pages are
  edge-cached for 60 seconds so the server cannot make this choice. Dollars are
  the visible default, so a failed lookup shows dollars.

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  DO NOT "CORRECT" THE $2,500 TO $3,000. IT IS DELIBERATE.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------

  On 2 September 2026 Ernest set a floor: nothing one-off under $3,000, because
  /global sells a build from $3,000 and roughly a thousand cold emails point at
  that page, so a cheaper one-off tier elsewhere undercuts the thing being
  advertised. That floor was applied to the four service page globals by
  cms/scripts/set-service-page-usd-prices.mjs.

  It never reached this page, because these prices are typed into this file
  rather than held in the CMS, and the script only writes to the CMS. That was
  an oversight, not a decision, and it was found on 2 September 2026 when the
  Fieldwork and homepage numbers were compared side by side.

  Shown the gap, Ernest kept $2,500. So this is now the second deliberate break
  in the floor, alongside the video Reel Pack at $1,250, and it is recorded here
  for the same reason that one is: an undocumented exception looks exactly like
  a mistake, and the next person to run a pricing pass will "fix" it.

  If the floor is ever enforced by a script, this tier and Reel Pack are the two
  it has to skip.
*/
const tiers = [
    {
        name: "Setting it up",
        price: "from $2,500",
        priceGhs: "from GH₵ 6,000",
        usdMin: 2500,
        cadence: "One off, two to three weeks",
        body: "Working out the definition, building and testing the search, and the first list delivered and checked by hand.",
    },
    {
        name: "Leads only",
        price: "from $300",
        priceGhs: "from GH₵ 900",
        usdMin: 300,
        cadence: "A month",
        body: "A fresh list, everything re-checked, duplicates and dead ends stripped out. You do your own outreach.",
    },
    {
        name: "Done for you",
        price: "from $1,500",
        priceGhs: "from GH₵ 4,000",
        usdMin: 1500,
        cadence: "A month",
        body: "Everything above, plus the emails written and sent, and the video and images made. You take the meetings.",
    },
];

const notList = [
    {
        lead: "It is not software.",
        body: "You are not buying a login and you are not being asked to run anything. If you wanted a tool you would already have bought one, and it would be sitting unused.",
    },
    {
        lead: "It is not a database.",
        body: "Nothing here is scraped from a list somebody else is also selling. That is the point, and it is also why it costs more than a seat on a platform.",
    },
    {
        lead: "It is not instant.",
        body: "The first month is slow because the definition has to be right. Months two onward are quick.",
    },
    {
        lead: "It is not unconditional.",
        body: "You get a loud voice pointed at real people, and my name is attached to how you use it. The contract says unsubscribes are honoured, no lists bought from anywhere else are mixed in, and nobody pretends to be someone they are not. If that is a problem, this is not the right service.",
    },
];

// Each answer has to stand alone, because answer engines quote them without the
// rest of the page. One array feeds the visible accordion and the schema, so the
// two cannot drift apart.
const faqs = [
    {
        q: "How is this different from a lead database?",
        a: "A database lets you filter on standard fields such as industry, job title and company size. Fieldwork starts from a description of your customer that no database has a field for, such as a business that recently opened a second location or one whose website has no mobile version, and finds those businesses by reading public information about them.",
    },
    {
        q: "How many businesses do I get?",
        a: "It depends on how narrow your definition is. A tight definition in a small market might return sixty a month and be worth more than six hundred loose ones. The number is agreed before you sign, not promised in advance.",
    },
    {
        q: "Are the email addresses verified?",
        a: "Yes. Every address is checked before delivery, and anything that bounced the previous month is removed from the next list.",
    },
    {
        q: "Can I buy a single list instead of subscribing?",
        a: "No. A list decays from the day it is made, and a one off sale means you get the blame for data that was accurate when it left. This is monthly or not at all.",
    },
    {
        q: "Who owns the data?",
        a: "You do. It is your list, delivered to you, and you can keep it if you stop.",
    },
    {
        q: "Do you send the emails as well?",
        a: "Only on the done for you tier, and only under the conduct rules in the contract.",
    },
];

/*
  Two nodes in one graph. The page carried only the FAQ, which was right while it
  was noindex and reachable by link alone. It is a listed, indexable service now,
  and nothing in the markup said what the service was or who provides it.

  The provider reuses the homepage's @id so this reads as the same business
  rather than a second one with a similar name.

  Prices are minimums, which is what the visible tiers now say too. Declaring a
  single fixed price for work that is quoted per client would be a claim the page
  itself contradicts two paragraphs later.
*/
const jsonLd = {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@graph": [
        {
            "@type": "Service",
            "@id": "https://quademdigital.com/services/fieldwork/#service",
            name: "Fieldwork",
            serviceType: "Prospect research and lead generation",
            description:
                "Monthly lists of businesses matching a customer definition no lead database has a field for, found by reading public information at scale and checked by hand.",
            provider: { "@id": "https://quademdigital.com/#business" },
            areaServed: ["GB", "US", "GH", "AE"],
            offers: tiers.map((t) => ({
                "@type": "Offer",
                name: t.name,
                description: t.body,
                priceSpecification: {
                    "@type": "PriceSpecification",
                    priceCurrency: "USD",
                    minPrice: t.usdMin,
                },
            })),
        },
        {
            "@type": "FAQPage",
            mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
                "@type": "Question",
                name: f.q,
                acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
            })),
        },
    ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Fieldwork: monthly prospect lists built to your definition"
    description="Lead tools only let you ask for boxes: industry, job title, company size. There is no box for a dentist who just opened a second branch. Fieldwork finds those businesses every month, checked by hand."
    activePage="services"
    navbarScrolled={true}
    jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
    <article class="fw">

        <header class="fw-hero">
            <div class="fw-col">
                <span class="fw-eyebrow">Fieldwork</span>
                <h1>You already know who your best customer is. I go and find the rest of them.</h1>
                <p class="fw-standfirst">
                    Every month you get a list of businesses that match your exact description, each
                    with a checked email address, a phone number, and one line explaining why they
                    need calling now. Not a login to a database everyone else already has.
                </p>
                <a class="fw-cta" href={CTA_HREF} data-track="fieldwork_cta">{CTA_LABEL}<span class="fw-cta-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8594;</span></a>
                <p class="fw-cta-note">
                    If you can describe them clearly, I can build this. If you cannot, you do not
                    have a lead problem and I will tell you so.
                </p>
            </div>
        </header>

        <section class="fw-section">
            <div class="fw-col">
                <h2><span class="fw-num">01</span>The list you want does not exist</h2>
                <p>
                    Lead tools work off standard boxes. Industry, job title, company size, location.
                    Tick the boxes, get your list. That is fine if your customer fits in a box.
                </p>
                <p><strong>Good customers never do.</strong></p>
            </div>

            <div class="fw-wide">
                <div class="fw-compare">
                    <div class="fw-compare-head fw-muted">What a lead tool lets you ask for</div>
                    <div class="fw-compare-head fw-accent">What you actually want</div>
                    {contrasts.map(([left, right]) => (
                        <>
                            <div class="fw-compare-cell fw-muted">{left}</div>
                            <div class="fw-compare-cell fw-accent">{right}</div>
                        </>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="fw-col">
                <p>
                    There is no box for anything in the right hand column. You cannot buy that list.
                </p>
                <p>
                    But every one of those can be worked out from information anybody can see.
                    Somebody just has to go and look, at scale, and know what they are looking for.
                    That is the whole service. Not a secret database. Looking properly, every month,
                    at the things other people cannot be bothered to check.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="fw-section">
            <div class="fw-col">
                <h2><span class="fw-num">02</span>What arrives</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="fw-wide">
                <div class="fw-tiles">
                    {arrives.map((t) => (
                        <div class="fw-tile">
                            <h3>{t.name}</h3>
                            <p>{t.body}</p>
                        </div>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="fw-col">
                <blockquote class="fw-quote">
                    The third one sounds like housekeeping. It is the reason clients stay. Everyone
                    who has bought leads before has been burned by paying twice for the same dead
                    names.
                </blockquote>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="fw-section">
            <div class="fw-col">
                <h2><span class="fw-num">03</span>How it works</h2>
                <ol class="fw-steps">
                    {steps.map((s) => (
                        <li>
                            <strong>{s.lead}</strong>
                            <span>{s.body}</span>
                        </li>
                    ))}
                </ol>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="fw-section">
            <div class="fw-col">
                <h2><span class="fw-num">04</span>Who this is for</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="fw-wide">
                <div class="fw-twocol">
                    <div class="fw-panel fw-panel-yes">
                        <h3>A good fit</h3>
                        <ul>{goodFit.map((i) => <li>{i}</li>)}</ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="fw-panel fw-panel-no">
                        <h3>Not a fit</h3>
                        <ul>{notFit.map((i) => <li>{i}</li>)}</ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="fw-section">
            <div class="fw-col">
                <h2><span class="fw-num">05</span>What it costs</h2>
                {/* Mandatory framing, and it goes above the prices, not below them.
                    Do not remove it. */}
                <p class="fw-framing">
                    These are starting points, not a rate card. What you pay depends on how awkward
                    your niche is to search, and I will tell you which of the three you actually
                    need rather than the most expensive one.
                </p>
                {/* Hidden until the country is known, then filled in by
                    initDynamicPricing. Baking a currency name into the HTML
                    would get it edge-cached and served to the wrong country. */}
                <p class="fw-framing" data-currency-note hidden>All prices in USD.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="fw-wide">
                <div class="fw-tiers">
                    {tiers.map((t) => (
                        <div class="fw-tier">
                            <h3>{t.name}</h3>
                            <p class="fw-price" data-market="international">{t.price}</p>
                            <p class="fw-price" data-market="ghana" hidden>{t.priceGhs}</p>
                            <p class="fw-cadence">{t.cadence}</p>
                            <p>{t.body}</p>
                        </div>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="fw-section">
            <div class="fw-col">
                <h2><span class="fw-num">06</span>What this is not</h2>
                <div class="fw-not">
                    {notList.map((item) => (
                        <p><strong>{item.lead}</strong> {item.body}</p>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        {/*
          Section 07, Proof. Rewritten 2 September 2026.

          WHAT CHANGED. This section used to say Fieldwork had never been sold
          and carried only the crawl study, because that was the only evidence
          in existence. Fieldwork has now been run twice for a paying client, a
          delivery and logistics operator in Accra, so the section carries the
          engagement in front of the crawl study. The crawl study stays, because
          it is the same machine pointed at a whole market instead of at one
          client's definition.

          WHAT HAS NOT CHANGED. This is still not the proof the spec is waiting
          for. The spec wants how many businesses were found, how many replied
          and how many became clients. Found now exists. Replied and became
          clients do not, because the client has reported no outcomes back. The
          section says that in its own first sentence rather than letting a
          reader assume otherwise. When the outcomes arrive they go in.

          FOUR RULES BIND THIS SECTION, AND THEY DO NOT EXPIRE.

          1. The client is not named. Permission to name has not been given in
             writing. "A delivery and logistics operator in Accra" is the agreed
             wording and it holds until that permission exists.
          2. No business from either list appears here, by name, handle, number,
             or as an anonymised example identifiable from its description. The
             lists are the client's paid asset and they carry personal contact
             data covered by the Data Protection Act 2012. This survives being
             given permission to name the client, because it is not the client's
             permission to give. The three exhibits were checked by eye against
             this rule before they were published.
          3. Every figure is counted, not estimated. The sources are the
             delivered workbooks, where the totals are live formulas rather than
             typed numbers. A figure that cannot be traced to a cell in one of
             those files does not belong on this page.
          4. Do not write "seven out of ten". An earlier draft of the portfolio
             plan used that line and the pool does not support it. Of 49
             businesses sourced in week one, 18 were sent and 31 were dropped,
             which is closer to six in ten than seven.

          The figures are marked up as text, not left inside the exhibit images.
          A number that only exists inside a JPEG cannot be read aloud, searched,
          translated or corrected. See the note at the top of
          src/components/FieldworkEvidence.astro.
        */}
        <section class="fw-section">
            <div class="fw-col">
                <h2><span class="fw-num">07</span>Where you can watch it run</h2>
                <p>
                    No client has reported results back yet, so there are no reply rates or won
                    figures on this page. Putting invented ones here would be the exact dishonesty
                    the service is sold against. What follows is the work itself, counted.
                </p>

                <h3 class="fw-sub">Two weeks in Accra</h3>
                <p>
                    Since August 2026 a delivery and logistics operator in Accra has been receiving
                    a weekly list of businesses that move goods to customers often enough to be
                    worth calling. Forty businesses have been sent so far across two lists, from
                    frozen food and pharmaceutical supply to scaffolding and school books, and no
                    business has been sent twice.
                </p>

                <dl class="fw-figures">
                    <div>
                        <dt>49</dt>
                        <dd>businesses sourced in the first week from Google, TikTok and Instagram</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt>18</dt>
                        <dd>of them could be shown to be trading, and were sent</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt>31</dt>
                        <dd>could not, and were not sent</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt>40</dt>
                        <dd>businesses delivered over two weeks, each with a dated signal behind it</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt>36</dt>
                        <dd>of the forty reachable on WhatsApp, which is how this market answers</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt>0</dt>
                        <dd>repeats, tracked against a register of every name ever surfaced</dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>

                <p>
                    The thirty one that were dropped are the point. Every one of them looked usable
                    in a search. They had listings, followers, a website, a phone number. None of
                    them could be shown to have done anything in public in the previous thirty
                    days, so none of them went on the sheet. That is the work being paid for, and
                    it is the part a database cannot do for you.
                </p>

                <h3 class="fw-sub">The paperwork itself</h3>
                <p>
                    Three documents from that engagement, opened up. A delivered row exactly as it
                    reached the client, the log of what was thrown away, and the full trail behind
                    one business from the search that found it to the sheet it went out on. Open
                    any of them and read it.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="fw-wide">
                <FieldworkEvidence />
            </div>

            <div class="fw-col">
                <h3 class="fw-sub">Where the method was wrong</h3>
                <p>
                    In the first week one wholesaler was held back because the newest thing that
                    could be found about it was eight months old. In the second week it turned up
                    trading actively under a separate wholesale account, posting every fortnight,
                    with four branch order lines and shipping to more than a thousand retailers. It
                    was the strongest prospect in the whole pool and the first check had missed it.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A business can look dead on the profile you find first and be busy on one you
                    have not found yet. The fix was to check for a second account before writing
                    anything off, and it is now part of the routine. This is on the page because a
                    method that never reports its own misses is not a method.
                </p>

                <h3 class="fw-sub">The same machine at full size</h3>
                <p>
                    In August 2026 it found 3,928 businesses listed on Google Maps across the UK,
                    the Gulf and the US, opened all of them, and checked eight things about each
                    homepage. 2,828 of the websites loaded at all. 42.9% of those had no HTTPS.
                </p>
                <p>
                    That is the same searching, the same checking by hand and the same removal of
                    dead ends described in section 03, pointed at a whole market instead of one
                    client's definition. The full method, the sample and its limits are on the
                    study, including the parts that make the headline look worse.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <a class="fw-inline-link" href="/blog/small-business-website-crawl-2026/">
                        Read the crawl study
                    </a>
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="fw-section">
            <div class="fw-col">
                <h2><span class="fw-num">08</span>Questions</h2>
                <div class="fw-faq">
                    {faqs.map((f) => (
                        <details>
                            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
                            <p>{f.a}</p>
                        </details>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="fw-section fw-close">
            <div class="fw-col">
                <h2>Tell me about the last five customers you were really glad to get</h2>
                <p>
                    What did they have in common? If you can answer that in a couple of sentences,
                    there is something here to build. Reply and tell me, and I will tell you
                    honestly whether this works for your market before either of us spends any
                    money.
                </p>
                <a class="fw-cta" href={CTA_HREF} data-track="fieldwork_cta">{CTA_LABEL}<span class="fw-cta-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8594;</span></a>
                <div class="fw-sign">
                    <p>Ernest</p>
                    <p>Founder, Quadem Digital</p>
                    <p><a href="mailto:ernest@quademdigital.com">ernest@quademdigital.com</a></p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

    </article>

    <ServiceEnquiryForm slug="fieldwork" returnTo="/services/fieldwork/" />

</BaseLayout>

<style>
    .fw { padding: 0 20px 100px; font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.68; }
    .fw-col { max-width: 660px; margin: 0 auto; }
    .fw-wide { max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; }
    .fw p { color: var(--text-muted); margin: 0 0 16px; }
    .fw strong { color: var(--text-primary); font-weight: 600; }

    /*
      A ground for the hero. Tokens and a gradient rather than an image, so it
      costs nothing to paint and the headline stays the Largest Contentful
      Paint. The page opened as bare type on a flat background, which reads as a
      document rather than as something built.
    */
    .fw-hero {
        padding: 80px 0 20px;
        position: relative;
    }
    .fw-hero::before {
        content: "";
        position: absolute;
        inset: -40px 0 0;
        z-index: -1;
        pointer-events: none;
        background:
            radial-gradient(56ch 38ch at 74% 12%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 14%, transparent), transparent 70%),
            radial-gradient(46ch 30ch at 4% 0%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 7%, transparent), transparent 72%);
